    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d820">

                <pb facs="0671" n="663" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg663"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6289: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-02-05T12:00:00+08:00">February 5,
                        1937—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received February 5—6:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>62. For Hornbeck.<anchor n="84" xml:id="d820-f1"/> My 386, January [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">December</hi>] 31, [1936] 11 a.m.<anchor n="83"
                        xml:id="d820-f2"/> In conversation with Kung<anchor n="85" xml:id="d820-f3"
                    /> last evening the latter asked me to ask you to use your influence to persuade
                    interested Americans to accept Chinese proposal for settling Hukuang bond loan.
                    Kung referred to question recently asked in Parliament concerning attitude of
                    the American bondholders and stated that it was true that Germans and British
                    have accepted the Chinese proposal, only the Americans holding out.</p>
                <p>Kung also expressed hope that something might be accomplished in regard to
                    Continental Bank loan while he is still in office.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d820-f1" n="84" xml:id="d820-f1-content">
                <p>Stanley K. Hornbeck, Chief of the Division of Far Eastern Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d820-f2" n="83" xml:id="d820-f2-content">
